BITMASK — REPRESENTING SUBSETS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

An integer can represent which elements from a set of n items
are "selected". Bit i is 1 if item i is included.

n items → 2^n possible subsets → masks from 0 to (1<<n) - 1

// Iterate over all subsets of n items
for (int mask = 0; mask < (1 << n); mask++) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (mask & (1 << i)) {
            // item i is in this subset
        }
    }
}

// Check if item i is in mask
bool inSubset = mask & (1 << i);

// Add item i to mask
mask |= (1 << i);

// Remove item i from mask
mask &= ~(1 << i);

// Count items in mask (size of subset)
int size = __builtin_popcount(mask);

// Iterate over all subsets of a given mask (advanced)
for (int sub = mask; sub > 0; sub = (sub - 1) & mask) {
    // process sub
}
// This is O(3^n) total across all masks — used in bitmask DP.
