COMMON PATTERNS IN LEETCODE PROBLEMS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Pattern 1 — XOR to find missing or duplicate
When every element appears an even number of times except one,
XOR of the whole array gives that element.
XOR with 1 to n gives the missing number.
  // Missing number in [0, n]
  int missing = n;
  for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) missing ^= i ^ nums[i];
  // LeetCode 268
Pattern 2 — Bitmask as visited state in DP
When n ≤ 20 and you need to track which items are "used",
an integer bitmask is your visited array.
  // Bitmask DP skeleton
  vector<int> dp(1 << n, INT_MAX);
  dp[0] = 0;
  for (int mask = 0; mask < (1 << n); mask++) {
      if (dp[mask] == INT_MAX) continue;
      for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
          if (mask & (1 << i)) continue;   // already used
          int newMask = mask | (1 << i);
          dp[newMask] = min(dp[newMask], dp[mask] + cost[i]);
      }
  }
Pattern 3 — AND of a range
AND of all numbers from l to r.
Trick: find the common prefix of l and r in binary.
  // LeetCode 201
  int shift = 0;
  while (l != r) { l >>= 1; r >>= 1; shift++; }
  return l << shift;
Pattern 4 — Counting bits for all numbers 0 to n
Use the fact that countBits(i) = countBits(i >> 1) + (i & 1).
  // LeetCode 338
  vector<int> dp(n + 1, 0);
  for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
      dp[i] = dp[i >> 1] + (i & 1);
Pattern 5 — Reverse bits
Reverse all 32 bits of an integer.
  // LeetCode 190
  uint32_t reverseBits(uint32_t n) {
      uint32_t res = 0;
      for (int i = 0; i < 32; i++) {
          res = (res << 1) | (n & 1);
          n >>= 1;
      }
      return res;
  }
